<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Long Biên Bridge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1902">1902</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Paul Doumer Bridge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-bien">Long Biên</a> Bridge is a cantilever railway and road bridge across the Red River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, built by the French between 1899 and 1902 and originally named after Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-doumer">Paul Doumer</a>. Part of the railway linking Hanoi with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiphong">Haiphong</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, it was the longest bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> when completed. It was repeatedly bombed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> and remains in use.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/long-bien-bridge.jpg" alt="Long Biên Bridge" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phunghung · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Long_Bi%C3%AAn_Bridge_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
